#---------------------------------------------------------------------------#
# Hide the RAID1 array from udisks, so it is only ever mounted by fstab.      #
#                                                                            #
# Without this, the array is offered in Dolphin (and anything else using      #
# Solid/udisks) as a removable volume. Clicking it mounts the *top level*     #
# subvolume -- subvolid=5 -- at /run/media/deck/<label>, alongside the fstab  #
# mount of @SATA at /home/deck/SATA. The same files are then reachable by     #
# two different paths, one of them with an extra @SATA/ component:            #
#                                                                            #
#     /home/deck/SATA/<game>                                                  #
#     /run/media/deck/SATA/@SATA/<game>                                       #
#                                                                            #
# That happened on 2026-08-02 and produced three simultaneous mounts          #
# (games, games1, games2) because udisks mounts *per device* and a two-device #
# array offers it the same filesystem twice, appending a suffix on each name  #
# collision. udisks then could not clean any of them up ("Device or resource  #
# busy") because it attributes the mount to /dev/sdb while /proc/mounts names #
# /dev/sda -- multi-device btrfs is outside udisks' model.                    #
#                                                                            #
# Two paths to one filesystem is a real hazard here, not just clutter: a game #
# launcher or a copy started against one path while Steam writes the other    #
# has no idea they are the same storage, and `install.sh --status` reports    #
# whichever mount it finds first.                                            #
#                                                                            #
# Matched on filesystem UUID, not /dev/sdX -- device letters are assigned in  #
# probe order and both mirror members must be covered.                        #
#                                                                            #
# Consequence, by design: the array no longer appears in the desktop's device #
# list at all. fstab mounts it at boot, so there is nothing to click. To      #
# mount it by hand (a degraded mirror, say) use mount(8) directly -- see      #
# README.md, "If a disk fails".                                               #
#---------------------------------------------------------------------------#
SUBSYSTEM=="block", ENV{ID_FS_UUID}=="@FS_UUID@", ENV{UDISKS_IGNORE}="1"
